Any solution (x, y) of David Park's system lies above the proposed solution (x₀, y₀). Thus (x₀, y₀) is the least solution, and x₀ equals (a ∪ b · a* · b)* · (c ∪ b · a* · d).

Each summand of the two equations is contained in the left-hand side. The second equation therefore makes y a prefixed point of the operator sending w to a · w ∪ (b · x ∪ d), so the least-fixed-point comparison and Arden's lemma put a* · (b · x ∪ d) below y. Associativity and left distributivity give (b · a* · b) · x ∪ b · a* · d = b · (a* · (b · x ∪ d)). With the summand inclusions, this shows that x is a prefixed point of the operator built from the feedback coefficient and the feedback constant. Arden's lemma identifies x₀ with the least fixed point of that operator, so x₀ lies below x. The same star inclusion, using x₀ below x, puts y₀ below y.
